# Template for android/keystore.properties. Chapter 30.
#
# Copy this file to `keystore.properties` (same directory) and fill in real values. That file is
# gitignored (see frontend/.gitignore's "SIGNING SECRETS" section) and must never be committed —
# app/build.gradle reads it only if present, so a machine without it still produces an unsigned
# release build rather than failing.
#
# THE KEYSTORE THIS CHAPTER GENERATED
#
# frontend/android/app/cartwise-release.keystore was generated this chapter with:
#
#   keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore app/cartwise-release.keystore -alias cartwise \
#     -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 \
#     -storepass cartwise-dev-keystore-pw -keypass cartwise-dev-keystore-pw \
#     -dname "CN=CartWise, OU=Development, O=CartWise, L=Unknown, ST=Unknown, C=IN"
#
# It signs the release AAB built and verified this chapter (see MOBILE.md), which is what proves
# the signing configuration actually works end to end. It is NOT the keystore that should sign a
# real Play Store submission:
#
#   - Its password is the literal string above, committed in this comment for reproducibility.
#     A keystore whose password is public in the repository's history protects nothing.
#   - THE KEYSTORE THAT SIGNS YOUR FIRST PLAY STORE UPLOAD MUST BE KEPT FOREVER. Play Console ties
#     an app's identity to the certificate that first signed it (Google Play App Signing changes
#     the mechanics slightly but the upload key still matters); losing it means an existing app can
#     never receive an update again, only be replaced by a new listing that starts at zero installs
#     and zero reviews. Generate a NEW keystore for the real submission, with a real random
#     password from a password manager, and back up the .keystore file and its password somewhere
#     that survives this laptop being reformatted — that is the whole requirement.
#
# keystorePassword=cartwise-dev-keystore-pw
# keyPassword=cartwise-dev-keystore-pw
# keyAlias=cartwise
# storeFile=cartwise-release.keystore
